{"causes":["Robert Kiyosaki's personal financial struggles inspired him to create a better financial education system","Sharon Lechter's expertise in business and law influenced the book's content and structure","The growing demand for financial literacy among young adults created an opportunity for Rich Dad Poor Dad to fill the gap"],"effects":["Inspired a new generation of entrepreneurs, investors, and financially literate individuals","Popularized the concept of 'asset-based wealth creation' and encouraged readers to rethink their relationship with money","Contributed to the global financial education movement by providing a fresh perspective on personal finance and wealth creation"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Robert-Kiyosaki","sourceName":"Robert Kiyosaki","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Sharon-Lechter","targetName":"Sharon Lechter","context":"Collaborated on the writing and content of Rich Dad Poor Dad"},{"sourceSlug":"Global Financial Education Movement","sourceName":"Global Financial Education Movement","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Rich-Dad-Poor-Dad","targetName":"Rich Dad Poor Dad","context":"Shaped the book's content and influenced its popularity"},{"sourceSlug":"Young Adult Market","sourceName":"Young Adult Market","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Rich-Dad-Poor-Dad","targetName":"Rich Dad Poor Dad","context":"Created a new audience for financial education and entrepreneurship"},{"sourceSlug":"Financial Literacy Movement","sourceName":"Financial Literacy Movement","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"Rich-Dad-Poor-Dad","targetName":"Rich Dad Poor Dad","context":"Contributed to the growth of a global movement focused on personal finance and financial education"},{"sourceSlug":"Global Economy","sourceName":"Global Economy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Rich-Dad-Poor-Dad","targetName":"Rich Dad Poor Dad","context":"Influenced the global economy by promoting financial literacy and entrepreneurship"}],"places":[{"name":"Asheville, North Carolina","role":"Birthplace of Robert Kiyosaki"},{"name":"Los Angeles, California","role":"Location where Rich Dad Poor Dad was written and first published"},{"name":"New York City, New York","role":"Platform for the book's launch and promotion"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Rich Dad Poor Dad","type":"Reference article","slug":"rich-dad-poor-dad"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2342159","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rich_Dad_Poor_Dad"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Rich Dad Poor Dad contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Rich Dad Poor Dad: What the Rich Teach Their Kids About Money That the Poor and Middle Class Do Not is a personal finance book by Robert T. Kiyosaki (b. 1947) and Sharon Lechter, first published in April 1997 by Tech Press Inc. (self-published) and subsequently by Warner Books/Business Plus. The book presents a series of financial lessons framed through the contrasting philosophies of Kiyosaki's two 'fathers': his biological father — the 'poor dad' — a highly educated government employee who followed conventional financial advice (get a good education, find a secure job, save money, buy a house) and died nearly penniless; and the father of his best friend — the 'rich dad' — who had minimal formal education but became wealthy through financial literacy, asset building, and entrepreneurship.

Rich Dad Poor Dad has become one of the bestselling personal finance books of all time — selling over 32 million copies in 109 languages (as of 2021) and spending more than six years on The New York Times bestseller list. Its core argument is that the traditional middle-class approach to money (work for wages, pay taxes, buy consumer goods and a home) perpetuates financial dependence, while financial freedom requires building assets that generate passive income (businesses, real estate, intellectual property) rather than working for earned income that is taxed at higher rates. The book introduced the concepts of the 'rat race', 'financial literacy', and 'passive income' to mass popular culture.

Rich Dad Poor Dad is highly controversial — critics (including financial journalist John Reed and economists) have disputed whether the 'rich dad' was a real person, questioned Kiyosaki's financial advice (particularly his advocacy of real estate investment and tax avoidance strategies), and noted that much of the book's advice is vague or potentially harmful. Nevertheless, its cultural impact is undeniable: it triggered a personal finance publishing phenomenon, inspired the Rich Dad franchise (games, courses, seminars), and fundamentally shifted public discourse about financial literacy, passive income, and entrepreneurship in the late 1990s and 2000s.","subjects":["Personal Finance","21st Century","Robert Kiyosaki","Financial Literacy","Self-Help","Entrepreneurship","Popular Culture","American Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Rich Dad Poor Dad has had a lasting impact on global financial education by promoting financial literacy and entrepreneurship among young adults.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
